I believe that there are some typo errors in the problem statement (although I may not have done this problem thoroughly). If not, please check my work:

We first start off by drawing a diagram as shown below:
[asy]
size(8cm);
draw(circle((0,0), 1));
draw((1/2, sqrt(3)/2) -- (1/2, 0) -- (0,0), purple);
draw((0,0) -- (0.3,0) -- (1/2, sqrt(3)/2) -- cycle, blue+purple);
markscalefactor=0.01;
draw(anglemark((0.3, 0), (0,0), (1/2, sqrt(3)/2)));
draw(rightanglemark((1/2, sqrt(3)/2), (1/2, 0), (0,0)));
label(" $\theta$ ", (0.075,0), NE);
label(" $Rd\theta$ ", (1/2, sqrt(3)/2), NE);
label(" $M$ ", (0.3, 0), S);
label(" $A$ ", (0,0), SW);
dot((0,0));
label(" $B$ ", (1/2, 0), SE);
dot((0.3, 0));
dot((1/2, 0));
draw(arc((0, 0), 1, 62, 58), linewidth(1.4)+red);
[/asy]
Let  $AM = a$  which means that  $MB = R\cos\theta - a$ . Let us call the point of intersection with the line segments and the ring to be  $C$ . It is then seen that  $\angle CMB = \phi$ . We desire to find  $dB\cos\phi$  so we must first express  $\cos\phi$  in terms of the other factors. We see then by trigonometry that 
\[\cos\phi = \frac{R\cos\theta - a}{r}\]
where  $r$  is the distance  $MC$ . We can find  $MC$  from the law of cosines or 
\[MC = \sqrt{R^2 + a^2 - 2aR\cos\theta}\implies \cos\phi = \frac{R\cos\theta - a}{\sqrt{R^2 + a^2 - 2aR\cos\theta}}.\]
We also note that 
\[\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{\hat{z}}\frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r}\implies B = \boxed{\frac{\mu_0 I}{\pi}\cdot \int_{0}^{\pi}\frac{R\cos\theta - a}{\sqrt{(R^2 + a^2 - 2aR\cos\theta)^3}}d\theta}.\]

