The area of an equilateral triangle is equal to  $\frac{s^2\sqrt{3}}{4}$ , where  $s$  is the side length of the triangle. (This can be derived from using the Pythagorean theorem.)

Substituting  $s$  as  $1$ , we have  $Area=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$ .

Since the area of the square is equal to  $s^2$ , where  $s$  is the side length, we can set up an equation to find  $s$ .

The area of the square is  $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$ . 

Therefore,

\begin{align*}
\displaystyle{s^2} &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} 
\sqrt{s^2} &= \sqrt{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}} 
s &= \frac{\sqrt[4]{3}}{2} = \boxed{\textbf{(A)}}
\end{align*}

