Very beautiful problem!

Define  $p=2017, q=73$ .  $f(x) = 1+x^1+x^4+x^9+...+x^{q^2}$ . Now note that for a fixed  $k\in\mathbb{N}$ , if we let  $f(x)^k=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}a_jx^j$ . Then we have
\begin{align*}T_k &= a_1 + a_{q+1}+a_{2q+1}+...
& = \dfrac{1}{q}\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}a_j(1+\omega^{j-1}+\omega^{2j-2}+...+\omega^{(q-1)j-(q-1)})
&=\dfrac{1}{q}\left(f(1)^k+\frac{f(\omega)^k}{\omega}+\frac{f(\omega^2)^k}{\omega^2}+...+\frac{f(\omega^{q-1})^k}{\omega^{q-1}}\right)
&=\dfrac{1}{q}\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}\dfrac{f(\omega^j)^k}{\omega^j}
\end{align*}
Where  $\omega=e^{\frac{2\pi i}{q}}$ . By the properties of quadratic gauss sum,  $f(\omega^j)^2=q$  for every  $j$ , not dividing  $q$  and  $f(1)=73$ . 

Working on  $\mathbb{Z}_p[\omega]$ , a finite field with characteristic  $p$ . By  $(a+b)^p=a^p+b^p$  and the fact that  $\left(\dfrac{p}{q}\right)=1$  (here we are using Legendre Symbol), it's easy to show that  $f(\omega^j)^p=f(\omega^j)$ . From discussion above, we seek  $f(\omega^j)\ne 1$ , therefore
\begin{align*}
T_1+T_2+...+T_p &= \dfrac{1}{q}\sum_{k=0}^{2017}\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}\dfrac{f(\omega^j)^k}{\omega^j}
&=\dfrac{1}{q}\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}\dfrac{f(\omega^j)}{\omega^j}(1+f(\omega^j)+f(\omega^j)^2+...+f(\omega^j)^{p-1})
&=\dfrac{1}{q}\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}\dfrac{f(\omega^j)(f(\omega^j)^p-1)}{\omega^j(f(\omega^j)-1)}
&=T_1 = 2.
\end{align*}
Hence the answer is  $\fbox{2}$ .

