dot(" $B$ ", B, dir(180));
dot(" $C$ ", C, dir(0));
dot(" $D$ ", D, dir(270));
draw(A--B--D--C--A, heavyred);
draw(B--C, heavyred);
path w1 = circle(B, abs(A - B));
path w2 = circle(C, abs(A - C));
draw(w1);
draw(w2);
pair E = dir(C - A)/dir(B - A)*(A - B) + B;
dot(" $E$ ", E, dir(135));
pair F = dir(B - A)/dir(C - A)*(A - C) + C;
dot(" $F$ ", F, dir(45));
draw(E--D--F, heavycyan);
pair P = extension(E, rotate(90, E)*B, F, rotate(90, F)*C);
dot(" $P$ ", P, dir(90));
draw(E--P--F, deepgreen);
draw(E--F, deepgreen);
draw(P--D, heavycyan);
[/asy]

First  $P$  is on the radical axis of the two circles, so  $PE = PF$ . Also \[\angle EPF = 180^\circ - \angle EDA - \angle ADF = \angle AED + \angle AFD = \angle ABC + \angle ACB = 90^\circ.\] So  $PEF$  is a  $45^\circ - 45^\circ - 90^\circ$  triangle. Also, by the extended law of sines  $AE = 2\cdot AB \cdot \sin 45^\circ = 20\sqrt{2}$  and  $AF = 21\sqrt{2}$ , which means  $PE = PF = 41$ . Finally,  by the law of cosines \[PA^2 = 41^2 + 20^2\cdot 2 - 41\cdot 20\sqrt{2}\cdot \sqrt{2} = 841,\] so  $PA = 29$ . So then  $[PBC] = \tfrac{20\cdot 21}{2} + \tfrac{29^2}{2} = \boxed{\tfrac{1261}{2}}$ .</details>

