Let  $CS\cap AB=D,OM\cap BS=F.$  

Let the circumcircles  $\omega_1,\omega_2$  of  $\triangle BSD$  and  $\triangle SOF$  respectively, meet again at  $E.$ We consider the spiral similarity  $\mathcal{S}$  centered at  $E,$  which sends  $\omega_1$  to  $\omega_2.$ Clearly,  $B,S,F$  are collinear, and as  $CA=CB,$  the points  $O,S,D$  are also collinear.

Thus,  $\mathcal{S}:D\mapsto F,$  and  $\mathcal{S}:B\mapsto O.$ This means,  $\angle OFS=\angle SDB=90^{\circ}\implies OM\perp BS.$ Hence the conclusion.  $\blacksquare$

